  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$49{t}^{12}+9=$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n49{t}^{12}+9={(7{t}^{6})}^{2}+{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{t}^{12}+9 & =49{t}^{12}-(-9) \\\\  & =49{t}^{12}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(7{t}^{6})}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(7{t}^{6}+3\\text{i})(7{t}^{6}-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
